    <!--
        THIS COMMENT SITS INSIDE THE ROOT ELEMENT DELIBERATELY. A comment placed ABOVE
        <CustomField> breaks `sf` deploy at source conversion with a misleading
        "unable to find matching parent xml file" error.

        =====================================================================================
        2026-08-19 - DISPOSITION FLOW REDESIGN. FOUR VALUES ADDED, THE MASTER ORDER REWRITTEN,
        THREE VALUES PARKED FOR DELETION.
        =====================================================================================

        ADDED: Broker Selection, Release Materials, Offer Selection, Sale Closes.
        DOOMED (still present, deliberately): Call for Offers, Disposition Offer, Completed.

        🔴 WHY THE THREE DOOMED VALUES ARE STILL HERE. A METADATA DEPLOY CANNOT DELETE A
        PICKLIST VALUE. Removing them from this file removes them from the SOURCE only; the org
        keeps them, and the next `sf project retrieve` UNIONS local and remote values and puts
        them straight back, which reads exactly like a failed deploy. Deletion is a MANUAL step
        in Setup (Object Manager > Disposition Stage > Del next to each value), and it is
        scheduled as a separate cleanup wave AFTER this deploy is verified. Until that wave
        runs, do not trim this list.
        ⚠ An org sweep on 2026-08-19 returned ZERO Disposition__c rows on all three doomed
        values and zero pending approval instances, so there is no data migration to protect and
        no window in which a row can be stranded. That is the ONLY reason the validation rules
        and path assistants ship in the SAME deploy as this change rather than a later one.

        =====================================================================================
        THE ORDERING ARITHMETIC - THIS IS A PER-FIELD CALCULATION AND IT WAS NOT AN APPEND.
        =====================================================================================
        Runtime Path order comes from THIS list's order (sorted = false), filtered by the record
        type. A record type can only INCLUDE or EXCLUDE a value; it can NEVER RE-ORDER one. So
        the master position of every value decides what BOTH paths render, and appending the
        four new values to the end would have rendered the on-market Path as
        "... Closing, Completed, Broker Selection, Release Materials, Offer Selection,
        Sale Closes" - the finish line in the middle and four steps trailing behind it.
        It would have deployed green and no Apex or Jest test could see it.

        Required final sequences:
          On-Market  (11): Readiness, BOV Outreach, Broker Selection, NDA, Release Materials,
                           Active Listing, Offer Selection, LOI, PSA, Closing, Sale Closes
          Off-Market  (9): Readiness, Broker Selection, NDA, Release Materials,
                           Offer Selection, LOI, PSA, Closing, Sale Closes

        Master positions written below, and each path's filtered walk:
          1 Disposition Readiness   On: 1  Off: 1
          2 BOV Outreach            On: 2
          3 Broker Selection        On: 3  Off: 2
          4 NDA                     On: 4  Off: 3
          5 Release Materials       On: 5  Off: 4
          6 Active Listing          On: 6
          7 Offer Selection         On: 7  Off: 5
          8 LOI                     On: 8  Off: 6
          9 PSA                     On: 9  Off: 7
         10 Closing                 On:10  Off: 8
         11 Sale Closes             On:11  Off: 9
        Both walks are monotonic. THE INSERTION WAS AN INTERLEAVE, NOT AN APPEND: NDA (was
        position 5) had to move AHEAD of Active Listing (was position 3), because NDA is now an
        ON-MARKET stage that precedes listing, and Release Materials had to land between them.

        The three doomed values are PARKED AT THE END (positions 12 to 14) rather than left
        where they were. They carry no records, so their position is cosmetic - but the record
        types keep them during the transition window, so they DO still render as Path steps.
        Parking them past Sale Closes keeps the three orphan steps behind the real terminal
        instead of scattered through the middle of both paths.

        ⚠ VERIFY THE ORDER IN SETUP AFTER DEPLOY. A picklist REORDER (as opposed to an ADD) is
        the one part of this file that is worth reading back: if the org keeps the old ordering,
        both Paths render wrong while every test still passes.

        =====================================================================================
        THE DOCTRINE THAT DIED WITH THIS CHANGE - READ THIS BEFORE "RESTORING" ANYTHING.
        =====================================================================================
        🔴 RETRACTED: "NDA and Disposition Offer are OFF-MARKET stage values ONLY." That
        sentence stood in On_Market.recordType-meta.xml, in Off_Market.recordType-meta.xml, in
        All_NDAs_Signed_Before_Progression's comment and in lwc/dispositionSidebar.js, and it is
        now FALSE. NDA is a stage on BOTH paths. So are Broker Selection, Release Materials,
        Offer Selection and Sale Closes.
        🔴 RETRACTED: "the two record types' stage value sets are DISJOINT" - they never were
        fully, and after this change they overlap on NINE of eleven values. The stage value
        alone NO LONGER IDENTIFIES THE PATH. Anything that needs to know which path a record is
        on must test the record type (see Disposition__c.Is_On_Market__c), not the stage.
        The one on-market-only survivors are BOV Outreach and Active Listing.

        RESTRICTED STAYS RESTRICTED. This is the field every stage gate, path, LWC router and
        approval entry criterion keys off; an ad-hoc value typed in by a user would route to
        nothing.
    -->
